Subject: [PATCH PROPOSED] ADT: make the CPU topology a parameter, not an assumption

DRAFT - proposal only. This is not in patches/xnu-12377/series and must not
be applied to the tree before F1 exists. See
docs/research/ios27/ADT-MULTICORE-PROPOSAL.md for the criteria, the
verification ledger and the reason it cannot land yet.

The Pong ADT contract refuses every topology but a single CPU:
scripts/pong_adt_contract.py:158-163 raises "Pong single-core ADT must expose
only /cpus/cpu0", and :189 reports 'cpu_count': 1 as a constant even if more
nodes were present. Because scripts/make-pong-adt.py:290-292 audits its own
output, an eight-core ADT cannot be produced at all today: --cpus 2 raises
before the file is written, and the only path that writes one is
--skip-image-verification, which also drops the Image4/nonce audit that the
same tree needs to boot a root volume.

The SM8475 is 1+3+4, and board.toml:210 still asks for eight CPUs, so the
single-core lock is a real limit on the ladder rather than a property of the
hardware. What must stay invariant is not the count: it is contiguous
cpu0..cpuN-1 naming, one MPIDR affinity per node (Aff1 = index, 0x000..0x700),
exactly one CPU in state running, and that CPU being MPIDR 0. Those four
checks are stricter than the single-core test they replace, and they are what
this draft adds.

scripts/pong_adt_contract.py
  audit_adt() gains expect_cpus=None: when the caller knows the requested
  count (the generator always does) the ADT must match it exactly.
  The topology block accepts 1..8 nodes, validates the four invariants above,
  and rejects an unknown state string instead of silently counting it.
  The report now carries cpu_count (measured), cpu_mpidrs and running_cpus,
  so a wrong topology shows up in adt-audit.json rather than in a guess.
  The checks list gains "CPU node count/affinity/state".

scripts/make-pong-adt.py
  The self-audit passes expect_cpus=args.cpus, which is what turns the
  generator's own gate from "one core or nothing" into "as many cores as the
  build asked for, validated".

Not touched on purpose: build-native-bootimg-local.py:139 runs the second
audit without expect_cpus (None -> any valid count). Passing args.cpus there
is a one-line follow-up, but it belongs with whoever changes the build
driver, not with this proposal.

Verified against a pristine copy of both scripts with this patch applied:
9/9 cases in k60-work/scratch/platform27/adt-multicore-selftest.py - 1/2/8
cores accepted (MPIDR 0x0 .. 0x700, one running), and refused for a count
mismatch, a second running CPU, a duplicate affinity, a stopped CPU0 and an
unknown state. Generated artefacts stay under k60-work/scratch; no image,
payload or kernel was built.

